Vale’s first request was the smallest one: rest your hands, neighbors.
   Eleven hundred pairs of hands went down like birds settling. A man
put his champagne on the floor to do it. A girl of about five sur‐
rendered her bread roll without being asked. Doña Fela, in her good
hat, studied her palms as though they had been lent to her.
   My own hand started downward.
   I caught it halfway and gave it the trolley handle to hold.
   “A few moments of listening,” said Vale.
   He let the quiet arrive. Behind him the house music thinned, one in‐
strument at a time, until only the low carrier stood in the room, a hun‐
dred and ten hertz under everything, a floor beneath the floor. Nothing
swelled and no light changed. Eleven hundred people had paid for
tasteful, and they were getting it.
   On the Garden, the color began to leave the edges of the screen and
go inward.
   I looked at the central platform. The ring of forty had their hands in
their laps, their eyes lowered. And from them, from forty throats and
forty closed mouths, a hum came up that I could feel in the soles of my
feet. It wasn’t loud. Each person was making a slightly different sound,
one rough, one nasal, one held far back in the chest, like a choir that
